              <span class="lr-compare-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </div>
            <label class="lr-range-label" for="map-scrubber"><span>Historic</span><span>Present</span></label>
            <input class="lr-range" id="map-scrubber" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="54" aria-label="Compare the historic and present-day maps" />
          </div>
          <div class="lr-evidence-copy">
            <p class="lr-eyebrow">What the map sequence tells us</p>
            <h3>Continuity is the clue.</h3>
            <p>The 1901 atlas labels a large A. C. Todd tract. The 1911 atlas labels a nearby waterbody “RESERVOIR.” The 1929–31 atlas shows that reservoir alongside a growing street plan. A county aerial from 1940 makes the pond visible inside the built neighborhood.</p>
            <div class="lr-evidence-list">
              <div><strong>1901</strong><span>A. C. Todd tract</span></div>
              <div><strong>1911</strong><span>Reservoir and dam-like feature</span></div>
              <div><strong>1930</strong><span>Water beside the street plan</span></div>
              <div><strong>1940</strong><span>Pond inside the neighborhood</span></div>
            </div>
            <p class="lr-small-note">This proves the waterbody persisted. It does not yet prove whether the original feature was natural, enlarged, excavated, or dammed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lr-map-strip">
