      <section class="lr-section lr-lake-section" id="lake" aria-labelledby="lake-title">
        <div class="lr-section-head">
          <span class="lr-section-number">05</span>
          <div>
            <p class="lr-eyebrow">The shared water</p>
            <h2 id="lake-title">The lake was never just scenery.</h2>
          </div>
          <p class="lr-section-note">It was an amenity, a budget, a calendar, and eventually a community institution.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lr-lake-grid">
          <figure class="lr-lake-plan"><img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1939-plot-plan.jpg" alt="1939 plot plan showing winding streets, homes, natural woods, and the lake" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The 1939 plot plan places the lake inside the winding-road neighborhood and labels a large area of natural woods.</figcaption></figure>
          <div class="lr-lake-copy">
            <p class="lr-display-copy">A private lake and playground helped make Westminster Ridge different from an ordinary subdivision.</p>
            <p>The 1939 account describes a community lake club purchasing the lake from the original subdividers. Members paid $2 per month for equipment, beach sand, and a lifeguard’s salary. Admission fees also helped finance the purchase.</p>
            <div class="lr-lake-facts"><span><strong>$2</strong><small>monthly dues</small></span><span><strong>1939</strong><small>club purchase underway</small></span><span><strong>today</strong><small>shared summer calendar</small></span></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="lr-lake-aerial"><img src="/public/lakenridge/images/1940-aerial.jpg" alt="1940 aerial view of the pond inside the neighborhood" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>1940 county aerial. The pond remains visible among the roads and houses.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="lr-lake-current"><img src="/public/lakenridge/images/lakenridge-letter.png" alt="2024 Lakenridge Club membership letter" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Current club materials preserve the shared lake calendar: opening day, cleanup, fishing, swimming, and summer events.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </section>

